Backup creation capability boundaries (2026-09-25)
=================================================

A successful configuration read is not evidence that a saved backup was created.
The generic pfSense adapter previously issued GET diagnostics/config_history/backup
and returned arbitrary provider data as "Backup created". Configuration XML strings
could bypass field-name redaction. Empty replies could also appear successful.

The low-level pfSense create_backup operation now raises a capability error before
network I/O. The adapter returns NOT_SUPPORTED without invoking the client or
including provider data. The firewall API maps this result to HTTP 501. The existing
permission, site visibility, deleted-gateway and anonymous access checks remain.
Neither read-only mode nor force arguments enable this unsupported operation.

The OPNsense/pfSense backup page no longer offers a create action that the adapters
do not implement. An English, Spanish and Chinese notice explains that creation,
history and configuration export are separate operations. History availability,
export, retry, and existing confirmed history revert/delete controls are preserved.
OpenWrt and MikroTik use separate page branches; this change does not qualify their
backup operations. Remaining per-operation capability presentation needs review.

Evidence and limits
-------------------
The inspected pfrest REST API v2.10.2 source, commit
 df41b63c0beaad21c8d96e8c6571c27173921172
contains GET/DELETE history revision(s) endpoints, not the backup endpoint called by
the previous implementation. This observation is limited to that inspected package;
it is not a claim about every pfSense release, plugin or deployment.
https://github.com/pfrest/pfSense-pkg-RESTAPI/tree/df41b63c0beaad21c8d96e8c6571c27173921172/pfSense-pkg-RESTAPI/files/usr/local/pkg/RESTAPI

36 new backend tests reproduce 24 failures in the previous implementation, with 12
passing controls. The corrected related suite passes 1,080 tests using an isolated
PostgreSQL 18.6 instance. Independent probes exercise 32 incoming TCP HTTP/PostgreSQL
cases and four low-level read controls, before and after, using real HTTPX and
verified TLS to an owned synthetic vendor server. The previous implementation
returned four synthetic configuration payloads and two provider diagnostics; the
corrected creation path returned none and made no backup request. Normal version
reads still worked. Identity, connection establishment and vendor responses are
fixtures; these checks do not prove vendor authentication or physical-device backup.

Eight new page tests reproduce six failures in the previous UI and preserve two
confirmation controls. All 897 frontend tests, lint and a strict production build
pass locally. Eight independent Chromium cases exercise the actual page and Axios
over loopback HTTP, including valid, empty and failed history and confirmed synthetic
revert/delete requests. There are no customer-device writes in these checks.

These local results must be bound to the sanitized public source and requalified in
the shipping images. They do not establish pfSense history/export interoperability,
managed backup storage, retention, restore integrity, RTO/RPO or enterprise readiness.
